from qcore import Q
T = "Antibacterial Drugs"
QUESTIONS = [
# ---------------- β-lactams ----------------
Q(T, "Penicillins: mechanism",
  "Penicillin G is bactericidal against Treponema pallidum and Streptococcus pyogenes. Which statement best describes its mechanism?",
  ["It structurally mimics D-Ala-D-Ala and binds transpeptidases (PBPs), blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking", "It binds D-Ala-D-Ala directly, blocking glycosyltransferase", "It inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase", "It binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and causes misreading of mRNA", "It inhibits dihydropteroate synthase"],
  "β-lactams bind and acylate penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases) and prevent cross-linking of peptidoglycan. Autolysins are also activated. They work best on actively dividing bacteria. Penicillin G (IV/IM) and V (oral) cover Gram-positive cocci and rods, Gram-negative cocci (meningococcus), and spirochetes. Penicillinases hydrolyze them. Vancomycin, by contrast, binds the D-Ala-D-Ala substrate itself."),
Q(T, "Penicillinase-resistant penicillins",
  "A patient has endocarditis caused by methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus that makes β-lactamase. Which drug is most appropriate?",
  ["Nafcillin", "Penicillin G", "Ampicillin", "Aztreonam", "Ceftazidime"],
  "Nafcillin, oxacillin, and dicloxacillin have bulky side chains that block access by staphylococcal penicillinase. They are drugs of choice for MSSA ('use naf for staph'), along with cefazolin. They are not active against MRSA, which has altered PBP2a. Nafcillin is cleared mainly by bile and can cause interstitial nephritis."),
Q(T, "Aminopenicillins: EBV rash",
  "A 17-year-old with fever, pharyngitis, and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy is given amoxicillin for presumed strep throat. Two days later she develops a diffuse, itchy maculopapular rash. What is the most likely explanation?",
  ["Amoxicillin-associated rash in infectious mononucleosis (EBV)", "IgE-mediated anaphylaxis to penicillin", "Scarlet fever from resistant group A strep", "Stevens-Johnson syndrome from amoxicillin", "Toxic shock syndrome"],
  "Most patients with EBV mononucleosis who receive ampicillin or amoxicillin develop a non-allergic maculopapular rash. It does not mean a true penicillin allergy. Aminopenicillins (ampicillin, amoxicillin) cover Gram-positives and some Gram-negatives ('HHEELPSS': H. influenzae, H. pylori, E. coli, Enterococci, Listeria, Proteus, Salmonella, Shigella). They are penicillinase-sensitive and are combined with clavulanate or sulbactam. Other toxicities: hypersensitivity and C. difficile colitis."),
Q(T, "Cephalosporins: generations",
  "Which cephalosporin has activity against MRSA because it binds PBP2a?",
  ["Ceftaroline", "Cefazolin", "Ceftriaxone", "Cefepime", "Cefoxitin"],
  "1st generation (cefazolin, cephalexin): Gram-positive cocci, Proteus, E. coli, Klebsiella ('PEcK'). Used for surgical prophylaxis and MSSA. 2nd (cefoxitin, cefuroxime): adds H. influenzae, Enterobacter, Neisseria, and anaerobes (cefoxitin). 3rd (ceftriaxone for meningitis and gonorrhea; ceftazidime for Pseudomonas): serious Gram-negatives. 4th (cefepime): Pseudomonas plus Gram-positives. 5th (ceftaroline): MRSA, but not Pseudomonas. Cephalosporins do not cover LAME: Listeria, Atypicals (Chlamydia, Mycoplasma), MRSA (except ceftaroline), Enterococci."),
Q(T, "Ceftriaxone: adverse effect",
  "A neonate with jaundice needs treatment for gonococcal conjunctivitis. Why is cefotaxime preferred over ceftriaxone in this infant?",
  ["Ceftriaxone displaces bilirubin from albumin and can cause kernicterus", "Ceftriaxone causes gray baby syndrome", "Ceftriaxone is not active against Neisseria", "Ceftriaxone causes cartilage damage", "Ceftriaxone is nephrotoxic in neonates"],
  "Ceftriaxone is highly protein-bound and displaces bilirubin. It also precipitates with calcium, so it is contraindicated with IV calcium in neonates. It can cause biliary sludging (pseudocholelithiasis) because of biliary excretion. Sulfonamides also cause kernicterus. Chloramphenicol causes gray baby syndrome."),
Q(T, "Carbapenems",
  "Imipenem is always given with cilastatin. What is the purpose of cilastatin, and what is a notable toxicity of imipenem?",
  ["It inhibits renal dehydropeptidase I; imipenem lowers the seizure threshold", "It inhibits β-lactamases; imipenem causes ototoxicity", "It increases CNS penetration; imipenem causes hyperkalemia", "It blocks tubular secretion; imipenem causes red man syndrome", "It inhibits CYP3A4; imipenem causes QT prolongation"],
  "Cilastatin inhibits renal dehydropeptidase I, which would otherwise break down imipenem in the tubules. Carbapenems (imipenem, meropenem, ertapenem, doripenem) are broad-spectrum and β-lactamase-resistant (including ESBL). They are reserved for serious infections. Imipenem is the most seizure-prone, and meropenem less so. Ertapenem does not cover Pseudomonas. Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (e.g., KPC) are a growing problem."),
Q(T, "Aztreonam",
  "A patient with a history of anaphylaxis to penicillin needs coverage for Pseudomonas aeruginosa pneumonia. Which β-lactam is least likely to cross-react?",
  ["Aztreonam", "Piperacillin", "Cefepime", "Imipenem", "Ampicillin"],
  "Aztreonam is a monobactam. Its single ring has minimal cross-reactivity with penicillins (except possibly ceftazidime, which shares a side chain). It binds PBP3 and covers only Gram-negative rods, including Pseudomonas, with no activity against Gram-positives or anaerobes. It is synergistic with aminoglycosides. It is usually nontoxic, with occasional GI upset."),
Q(T, "β-lactamase inhibitors",
  "Why is amoxicillin combined with clavulanic acid for a human bite wound?",
  ["Clavulanate inhibits β-lactamases made by oral anaerobes, S. aureus, and Eikenella, protecting amoxicillin", "Clavulanate blocks renal secretion of amoxicillin, raising levels", "Clavulanate has its own strong activity against MRSA", "Clavulanate inhibits bacterial protein synthesis", "Clavulanate prevents amoxicillin-induced diarrhea"],
  "β-lactamase inhibitors (clavulanate, sulbactam, tazobactam, avibactam) bind and inactivate many β-lactamases ('CAST'). This extends coverage to β-lactamase-producing S. aureus (MSSA), H. influenzae, Moraxella, and anaerobes (Bacteroides), which is ideal for bite wounds. Probenecid is the drug that blocks renal tubular secretion of penicillins."),
Q(T, "Daptomycin",
  "A patient with MRSA right-sided endocarditis and septic pulmonary emboli is switched to daptomycin. Later he develops MRSA pneumonia that is not from emboli. Why is daptomycin not used for pneumonia, and which lab value should be monitored?",
  ["It is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant; monitor CPK for myopathy", "It cannot penetrate lung tissue due to size; monitor ALT", "It causes bronchospasm; monitor peak flow", "It is a prodrug activated only in the heart; monitor troponin", "It concentrates in bile; monitor bilirubin"],
  "Daptomycin is a lipopeptide that inserts into Gram-positive cell membranes and depolarizes them. It is used for MRSA and VRE bacteremia, endocarditis, and skin infections. Pulmonary surfactant inactivates it, so it fails in pneumonia. Adverse effects: myopathy and rhabdomyolysis (monitor CPK, hold statins) and eosinophilic pneumonia."),
# ---------------- Protein synthesis inhibitors ----------------
Q(T, "Aminoglycosides: mechanism & toxicity",
  "A 70-year-old man receiving gentamicin plus ampicillin for enterococcal endocarditis develops rising creatinine and high-frequency hearing loss. What is the mechanism of gentamicin, and why doesn't it work against anaerobes?",
  ["It binds the 30S subunit and blocks initiation complex formation and causes mRNA misreading; uptake requires oxygen-dependent transport", "It binds the 50S subunit and blocks translocation; anaerobes have efflux pumps", "It inhibits DNA gyrase; anaerobes lack gyrase", "It inhibits cell wall synthesis; anaerobes lack peptidoglycan", "It binds 30S and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA; anaerobes lack ribosomes"],
  "Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin, neomycin, streptomycin) bind the 30S subunit, causing mRNA misreading and blocking the initiation complex. They are bactericidal, concentration-dependent, and have a post-antibiotic effect. Uptake needs O2, so they fail against anaerobes. Toxicities: nephrotoxicity (acute tubular necrosis), ototoxicity (especially with loop diuretics), neuromuscular blockade (avoid in myasthenia), and teratogenicity. Resistance comes from bacterial transferases that acetylate, phosphorylate, or adenylate the drug."),
Q(T, "Tetracyclines",
  "A 25-year-old is prescribed doxycycline for Lyme disease. Which counseling point is most important?",
  ["Avoid taking it with milk, antacids, or iron, and use sun protection", "Expect orange discoloration of urine", "Avoid alcohol because of a disulfiram-like reaction", "Monitor for red-green color blindness", "Take with high-dose calcium to prevent tooth staining"],
  "Tetracyclines (doxycycline, minocycline, tetracycline) bind the 30S subunit and block aminoacyl-tRNA attachment at the A site. They are bacteriostatic. Divalent cations (Ca2+, Mg2+, Fe2+, Al3+) chelate them and prevent absorption. Adverse effects: photosensitivity, GI upset, pill esophagitis, and tooth discoloration and bone growth inhibition in children and pregnancy. Doxycycline is cleared in feces and is safe in kidney disease. Uses: rickettsiae, Borrelia, Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, MRSA skin infections, acne."),
Q(T, "Macrolides",
  "A patient on azithromycin for community-acquired pneumonia is also taking methadone and ondansetron. Which adverse effect is of most concern, and what is azithromycin's mechanism?",
  ["QT prolongation; binds 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit to block translocation", "Ototoxicity; binds the 30S subunit", "Tendon rupture; inhibits DNA gyrase", "Aplastic anemia; inhibits peptidyl transferase", "Hyperkalemia; blocks ENaC"],
  "Macrolides (azithromycin, clarithromycin, erythromycin) bind the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and block translocation. Adverse effects ('MACRO'): GI Motility (motilin agonist; erythromycin is used for gastroparesis), Arrhythmia from long QT, Cholestatic hepatitis, Rash, and eOsinophilia. Clarithromycin and erythromycin inhibit CYP3A4. Resistance comes from methylation of 23S rRNA (erm) or efflux (mef). Uses: atypical pneumonia, pertussis, chlamydia, MAC."),
Q(T, "Clindamycin",
  "A patient with a lung abscess after aspiration (poor dentition, alcohol use disorder) is treated with clindamycin. Which of the following is a well-known complication of this drug?",
  ["Clostridioides difficile colitis", "Gray baby syndrome", "Disulfiram-like reaction", "Tendon rupture", "Photosensitivity"],
  "Clindamycin binds the 50S subunit and blocks peptide transfer. It covers anaerobes 'above the diaphragm' (aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, oral infections), invasive GAS (toxin suppression), and some community MRSA. It is among the drugs most associated with C. difficile. Metronidazole is preferred for anaerobes below the diaphragm."),
Q(T, "Linezolid",
  "A patient with VRE bacteremia is started on linezolid. He also takes sertraline. Two weeks later he develops agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and clonus. Labs show thrombocytopenia. What is the mechanism of this interaction?",
  ["Linezolid is a weak monoamine oxidase inhibitor, causing serotonin syndrome with SSRIs", "Linezolid inhibits CYP2D6, raising sertraline levels", "Linezolid blocks dopamine receptors, causing neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Linezolid inhibits GABA-A receptors", "Linezolid causes hyponatremia through SIADH"],
  "Linezolid binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and prevents formation of the 70S initiation complex. It covers MRSA and VRE. It weakly inhibits MAO, so it can cause serotonin syndrome with SSRIs and hypertensive reactions with tyramine. Prolonged use causes myelosuppression (especially thrombocytopenia), peripheral and optic neuropathy, and lactic acidosis. Resistance comes from point mutations in 23S rRNA."),
Q(T, "Chloramphenicol",
  "A drug that blocks the peptidyltransferase of the 50S ribosomal subunit is used in resource-limited settings for bacterial meningitis. Which toxicities limit its use?",
  ["Dose-dependent anemia, idiosyncratic aplastic anemia, and gray baby syndrome", "Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity", "Tendon rupture and QT prolongation", "Hyperkalemia and kernicterus", "Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy"],
  "Chloramphenicol inhibits 50S peptidyltransferase. It is bacteriostatic but penetrates the CNS well (meningitis, RMSF). It causes dose-dependent bone marrow suppression and rare irreversible aplastic anemia. Neonates lack UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, so the drug builds up and causes gray baby syndrome (cyanosis, vomiting, cardiovascular collapse)."),
Q(T, "Protein synthesis: subunit targets",
  "Which list correctly places protein synthesis inhibitors on their ribosomal subunits?",
  ["30S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines; 50S: chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid, macrolides, streptogramins", "30S: macrolides, clindamycin; 50S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines", "30S: linezolid, tetracyclines; 50S: aminoglycosides, macrolides", "30S: chloramphenicol, aminoglycosides; 50S: tetracyclines, linezolid", "All bind the 50S subunit"],
  "'Buy AT 30, CCEL (sell) at 50.' 30S: Aminoglycosides and Tetracyclines. 50S: Chloramphenicol, Clindamycin, Erythromycin (macrolides), Linezolid, and streptogramins. Aminoglycosides are bactericidal. Most of the others are bacteriostatic, except that linezolid is bactericidal against streptococci and streptogramins are bactericidal. Mupirocin inhibits isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase."),
# ---------------- Antifolates ----------------
Q(T, "TMP-SMX: mechanism",
  "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole blocks two sequential steps of bacterial folate synthesis. Which enzymes do the two drugs inhibit?",
  ["Sulfamethoxazole: dihydropteroate synthase; trimethoprim: dihydrofolate reductase", "Sulfamethoxazole: dihydrofolate reductase; trimethoprim: thymidylate synthase", "Both inhibit dihydrofolate reductase", "Sulfamethoxazole: DNA gyrase; trimethoprim: RNA polymerase", "Sulfamethoxazole: PABA synthesis; trimethoprim: dihydropteroate synthase"],
  "Sulfonamides are PABA analogs that inhibit dihydropteroate synthase. Trimethoprim inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase. Together they are bactericidal. Mammalian cells use dietary folate, so they are spared. Uses: UTI, Pneumocystis treatment and prophylaxis, toxoplasmosis prophylaxis, Nocardia, community MRSA skin infections, Shigella, and Salmonella."),
Q(T, "TMP-SMX: adverse effects",
  "A 60-year-old man with chronic kidney disease on lisinopril is started on TMP-SMX for a UTI. Five days later his potassium is 6.3 mEq/L and his creatinine has risen slightly. What is the mechanism of the hyperkalemia?",
  ["Trimethoprim blocks the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the collecting duct, like amiloride", "Sulfamethoxazole causes type IV renal tubular acidosis by destroying the adrenal gland", "Trimethoprim stimulates aldosterone secretion", "Rhabdomyolysis", "Sulfamethoxazole causes hemolysis that releases potassium"],
  "Trimethoprim blocks ENaC in principal cells, the same action as amiloride, causing hyperkalemia, especially with ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or CKD. It also blocks tubular creatinine secretion, raising serum creatinine without a true fall in GFR. Other TMP-SMX toxicities: hypersensitivity and SJS/TEN, hemolysis in G6PD deficiency, megaloblastic anemia and pancytopenia, kernicterus in neonates, and increased warfarin effect (CYP2C9 inhibition)."),
# ---------------- Fluoroquinolones ----------------
Q(T, "Fluoroquinolones",
  "A 72-year-old man taking prednisone for COPD is treated with levofloxacin for pneumonia. Ten days later he feels a sudden 'pop' in his heel and cannot plantarflex. What is the mechanism of levofloxacin, and what is the risk factor here?",
  ["Inhibits DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV; corticosteroids and age increase tendon rupture risk", "Inhibits RNA polymerase; corticosteroids increase hepatotoxicity", "Blocks the 30S subunit; corticosteroids increase ototoxicity", "Inhibits cell wall synthesis; age increases rash", "Forms free radicals that damage DNA; corticosteroids increase neuropathy"],
  "Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin) inhibit topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase) and topoisomerase IV. They are bactericidal. Toxicities: tendinopathy and tendon rupture (worse over age 60, with steroids, or with kidney disease), QT prolongation, cartilage damage (avoided in children and pregnancy), CNS effects (seizures, delirium), peripheral neuropathy, dysglycemia, and aortic aneurysm. Divalent cations reduce absorption. Resistance comes from gyrase mutations (gyrA) and efflux pumps."),
# ---------------- Metronidazole ----------------
Q(T, "Metronidazole",
  "A patient being treated for Trichomonas vaginalis drinks alcohol at a party and develops flushing, headache, nausea, vomiting, and tachycardia. What is the mechanism of this reaction and of the drug's antimicrobial action?",
  ["Inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase; forms toxic free radicals that damage DNA in anaerobes", "Inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase; inhibits 30S subunit", "Induces CYP2E1; inhibits DNA gyrase", "Causes serotonin release; inhibits folate synthesis", "Blocks histamine metabolism; inhibits cell wall synthesis"],
  "Metronidazole is a prodrug reduced by anaerobic and protozoal enzymes into toxic free radicals that damage DNA. It treats Giardia, Entamoeba, Trichomonas, bacterial vaginosis (Gardnerella), anaerobes below the diaphragm (Bacteroides, C. difficile), and H. pylori ('GET GAP on the Metro'). It causes a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol, a metallic taste, and peripheral neuropathy with long use."),
# ---------------- Others ----------------
Q(T, "Nitrofurantoin",
  "An 80-year-old woman has taken nitrofurantoin daily for years to prevent recurrent UTIs. She now has progressive dyspnea and bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Why is nitrofurantoin also inappropriate for pyelonephritis?",
  ["It reaches therapeutic levels only in the urine and not in renal tissue or blood", "It is inactivated in acidic urine", "It is excreted mainly in bile", "It only covers Gram-positive organisms", "It is a prodrug activated only in the liver"],
  "Nitrofurantoin is reduced by bacterial flavoproteins to reactive intermediates that damage ribosomes and DNA. It concentrates in urine and treats only uncomplicated cystitis. It does not cover Proteus or Pseudomonas. Chronic use can cause pulmonary fibrosis and hepatitis. It causes hemolysis in G6PD deficiency and should be avoided at low GFR."),
Q(T, "Polymyxins",
  "A carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii infection in the ICU is treated with colistin. What is its mechanism, and what are its main toxicities?",
  ["Cationic detergent that binds lipid A and disrupts Gram-negative membranes; nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity", "Binds D-Ala-D-Ala; ototoxicity", "Inhibits 50S; myelosuppression", "Inhibits DNA gyrase; tendinopathy", "Blocks folate synthesis; hyperkalemia"],
  "Polymyxin B and colistin (polymyxin E) are cationic polypeptides. They bind LPS and membrane phospholipids like a detergent, making the outer and inner membranes leaky. They are last-resort drugs for multidrug-resistant Gram-negatives (Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales). Toxicities: nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity (paresthesias, neuromuscular blockade, respiratory arrest). Plasmid-mediated mcr-1 modifies lipid A and confers resistance."),
Q(T, "Antibiotics in pregnancy",
  "A pregnant woman at 20 weeks has an uncomplicated E. coli UTI. Which antibiotic is contraindicated in pregnancy?",
  ["Doxycycline", "Cephalexin", "Amoxicillin-clavulanate", "Nitrofurantoin in the second trimester", "Fosfomycin"],
  "Avoid in pregnancy ('SAFe Children Take Really Good Care'): Sulfonamides (kernicterus near term), Aminoglycosides (ototoxicity), Fluoroquinolones (cartilage), Clarithromycin (embryotoxic), Tetracyclines (teeth and bone), Ribavirin (teratogen), Griseofulvin, Chloramphenicol (gray baby). β-lactams and fosfomycin are safe. Nitrofurantoin is acceptable in the second trimester but avoided at term (hemolysis in the fetus)."),
Q(T, "Bacteriostatic vs bactericidal",
  "Which pair of antibiotics consists of two drugs that are generally bactericidal?",
  ["Fluoroquinolones and aminoglycosides", "Tetracyclines and macrolides", "Clindamycin and sulfonamides", "Chloramphenicol and tetracyclines", "Macrolides and clindamycin"],
  "Bactericidal: β-lactams, vancomycin, daptomycin, aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, metronidazole, and TMP-SMX together. Bacteriostatic: tetracyclines, macrolides, clindamycin, chloramphenicol, sulfonamides or trimethoprim alone, and linezolid (except against streptococci). Bactericidal drugs are preferred for endocarditis, meningitis, and neutropenic patients. Aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones kill in a concentration-dependent way. β-lactams kill in a time-dependent way (time above MIC)."),
Q(T, "Resistance mechanisms",
  "Which pairing of antibiotic and resistance mechanism is correct?",
  ["Aminoglycosides — enzymatic acetylation, adenylation, or phosphorylation", "Vancomycin — β-lactamase production", "Fluoroquinolones — methylation of 23S rRNA", "Macrolides — D-Ala-D-Lac substitution", "Tetracyclines — altered PBPs"],
  "Common mechanisms: β-lactams — β-lactamases, altered PBPs (MRSA, pneumococcus), porin loss. Aminoglycosides — modifying enzymes. Vancomycin — D-Ala-D-Lac. Macrolides — methylation of 23S rRNA (erm), efflux. Tetracyclines — efflux pumps, ribosomal protection. Fluoroquinolones — gyrase and topo IV mutations, efflux, plasmid qnr. Sulfonamides — altered dihydropteroate synthase, more PABA. Rifampin — rpoB mutation."),
Q(T, "Rifampin monotherapy",
  "Why is rifampin never used alone to treat an active bacterial infection?",
  ["Single-step mutations in the RNA polymerase β-subunit (rpoB) quickly produce resistance", "It is inactive in the body without a second drug", "It is only bacteriostatic", "It cannot reach intracellular organisms", "It is inactivated by β-lactamases"],
  "Rifampin (and rifabutin) inhibits bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Resistance arises quickly from point mutations in rpoB. It is used alone only for short-term prophylaxis (meningococcal and Hib contacts). Rifampin strongly induces CYP450, turns body fluids orange, and can cause hepatitis. It is combined with other drugs for TB, leprosy, and prosthetic device infections (penetrates biofilm)."),
Q(T, "Endocarditis prophylaxis",
  "Which patient should receive amoxicillin before a dental extraction to prevent infective endocarditis?",
  ["A 60-year-old with a prosthetic aortic valve", "A 30-year-old with mitral valve prolapse without regurgitation", "A 25-year-old with a surgically repaired atrial septal defect 10 years ago", "A 50-year-old with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy", "A 40-year-old undergoing colonoscopy"],
  "Prophylaxis is given only for dental procedures that manipulate gingival tissue in high-risk patients. These include prosthetic valves or valve repair material, prior infective endocarditis, certain congenital heart disease (unrepaired cyanotic, recently repaired with prosthetic material, repaired with residual defects), and cardiac transplant recipients with valvulopathy. Amoxicillin is given 30–60 minutes before the procedure. GI and GU procedures do not routinely require prophylaxis."),
Q(T, "Antibiotic prophylaxis: HIV",
  "A patient with newly diagnosed HIV has a CD4 count of 80/μL and a positive Toxoplasma IgG. Which prophylaxis regimen is indicated?",
  ["TMP-SMX to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia and Toxoplasma encephalitis", "Azithromycin to prevent MAC", "Fluconazole to prevent Cryptococcus", "Valganciclovir to prevent CMV", "Isoniazid to prevent TB regardless of TST"],
  "HIV prophylaxis thresholds: CD4 <200 → TMP-SMX for Pneumocystis (dapsone or atovaquone if allergic). CD4 <100 with positive Toxoplasma IgG → TMP-SMX (already covers both). CD4 <50 → azithromycin for MAC only if ART is not started right away. Primary prophylaxis for CMV or Cryptococcus is not routine. The most important step is starting ART."),
Q(T, "Fosfomycin & bacitracin: cell wall steps",
  "Place the cell wall inhibitors in order of the step they block, from cytoplasmic precursor synthesis to cross-linking.",
  ["Fosfomycin (MurA) → bacitracin (bactoprenol recycling) → vancomycin (D-Ala-D-Ala) → β-lactams (transpeptidation)", "Vancomycin → fosfomycin → β-lactams → bacitracin", "β-lactams → vancomycin → bacitracin → fosfomycin", "Bacitracin → β-lactams → fosfomycin → vancomycin", "Fosfomycin → β-lactams → bacitracin → vancomycin"],
  "Fosfomycin inhibits MurA (enolpyruvate transferase), the first step in making UDP-N-acetylmuramic acid. It is a single-dose oral treatment for cystitis. Bacitracin blocks dephosphorylation of the bactoprenol lipid carrier. It is topical only, because it is nephrotoxic. Vancomycin binds the D-Ala-D-Ala of precursors to block glycosyltransferase and transpeptidation. β-lactams inhibit PBP transpeptidases that cross-link the wall."),
]
